    "node_id": "MDI0OlB1bGxSZXF1ZXN0UmV2aWV3Q29tbWVudDg1ODg3OTMw",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -7241,6 +7241,19 @@ a.setAttribute('href', 'http://example.com/'); // change the content attribute d\n   the number as a <span>valid non-negative integer</span> and that string must be used as the new\n   content attribute value.</p> <!-- see previous paragraph for note about the weird range -->\n \n+  <p>If a reflecting IDL attribute has an unsigned integer type (<code\n+  data-x=\"idl-unsigned-long\">unsigned long</code>) that is <dfn>clamped to the range</dfn>\n+  [<var>min</var>, <var>max</var>], then on getting, the content attribute must first be parsed\n+  according to the <span>rules for parsing non-negative integers</span>, and if that is successful,\n+  and the value is between <var>min</var> and <var>max</var> inclusive, the resulting value must be\n+  returned.  If it fails or returns a negative value, the default value must be returned.  If it\n+  succeeds but the value is a nonnegative value less than <var>min</var>, <var>min</var> must be\n+  returned.  If it succeeds but the value is greater than <var>max</var>, <var>max</var> must be\n+  returned.  On setting, it behaves the same as setting a regular reflected unsigned integer.</p>\n+  <!-- The above is a bit of a hack to support the fact that rowspan currently allows 0, but we want\n+  negative numbers to become 1 to match all browsers' behavior.  If this ever changes, we can remove\n+  the special case for a negative value. -->",
